 Given a triangle with base \( b \) and height \( h \), and a rectangle of height \( x \) inscribed in the triangle with its base in the base of the triangle, we start by noting that the triangles on either side of the rectangle are similar to the main triangle. Specifically, the smaller triangle above the rectangle has height \( h - x \) and is similar to the main triangle. Since the triangles are similar, the ratio of their corresponding sides is the same, so we have: \[
\frac{b - \text{base of smaller triangle}}{b} = \frac{h - x}{h}
\]
This simplifies to: \[
\text{base of smaller triangle} = b \left( 1 - \frac{h - x}{h} \right) = b \left( \frac{x}{h} \right) = \frac{bx}{h}
\]
The area of the rectangle is its height \( x \) times its base \( \frac{bx}{h} \). Therefore, the area of the rectangle is: \[
\text{Area} = x \left( \frac{bx}{h} \right) = \frac{bx^2}{h}
\]
